Z||000021||ChadMan||07-19-2001||10:01 AM||||Thanks, everybody.  I still have quite a few anger-management issues though - just not at my poor old neighbor.  I may offer to buff the scuff off his Corolla while I'm at it...not because I'm a nice guy...but because I can't stand to see my med red metallic paint on his car every morning.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 07-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||LZeitgeist||07-19-2001||10:11 AM||||If he'd denied it or lied about it, I'd definitely have put the screws to him, old and frail or not - but since he admitted blame, and accepted the responsibility for what had happened, I think you did right to let it go...<P>If all it takes is some buffing to get rid of it, then it's probably a job better fixed by your own hands anyway, as opposed to having him pay for professional repairs to the car. If it was worse (as in requiring a new body panel, repainting to match the rest of the car, etc.), then if it were me, I'd be more inclined to work out a deal with the guy for at least partial reimbursement for repairs, but that wasn't the case in this situation, so I think you done good.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>It feels good to be able to honestly and completely forgive someone, doesn't it? It's a wonderful-feeling release... almost a natural high, don'tcha think? If it's grudgingly given and more of a 'letting it slide' than an actual forgiveness, then it doesn't really feel the same... not sure which one of these this is for you this time - only you know for sure. (And either way, it's nobody's business but your own as to which way it feels for you.)<P>I'm trying to help myself reach that plateau more often myself...  ||||reg||1
Z||000023||Wannabe||07-20-2001||12:07 AM||||Good job Chadman. I really doesn't sound like he intended harm, and so does not fall into the same category as the ignorant SOB (polite) who threw and egg at my car tonight.  It must have been thrown from a passing car because the shell left a mark in the paint. Fortunatley it's white, so it's not visible. But what if the car had been black, or red or something? Why are there jerks out there who have to get their jolies this way? What did the car ever do to him, or to any of the other ignorant vandals out there. I've completely lost tolerance for that type of crap. People like that really should be....well, it's probably not a good idea to go into detail on public forum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000024||Mach10||07-20-2001||02:33 AM||||I'm almost the same way. If you so much as put the tiniest scratch on the door of my car, I expect you to own up to it if I catch you. Will I make you pay for it? Nah. I appreciate the honesty more than the work. If you are cowardly enough to hit and run without even taking some responsibility for it, then I will find you, and damn well make you pay for it. If you admit the fault, unless it's some serious damage that can't be fixed without major work, I'll probably let it slide.<P>That's a good thing you did.||||reg||6
